---
import CodeWindow from "../components/CodeWindow.astro";
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
import WhoWins from "../components/WhoWins.astro";
const { playful = true } = Astro.props;
---

<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span gradient>Let's go further</span>
    <span aria-hidden="true">ᯓ🏃🏻‍♀️‍➡️</span>
  </h3>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <CodeWindow is:raw lang="js" size="full">
    self.onmessage = (event) => {};
  </CodeWindow>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <CodeWindow is:raw lang="js" size="big">
    self.onmessage = (event) => {};
  </CodeWindow>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid" style="margin-block-start: 0.5em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      📌 data
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      📌 origin
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from below">
      📌 source
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      📌 ports
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      <b is:raw>-->all of these properties are read-only<--</b>
    </p>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        data: The data sent from the main thread to the web worker
      </li>
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        origin: The origin of the sender. For dedicated workers it is an empty
        string: it only matters for window/iframe messaging
      </li>
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        source: Represents the context that sent the message. In the case of Web
        Workers, this property is usually null because messages come from the
        main thread or other Workers, not from windows or iframes
      </li>
      <li style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
        ports: An array of MessagePort objects associated with the message.
        MessagePort are used for advanced two-way communication between threads
      </li>
    </ul>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <CodeWindow is:raw lang="js" size="full">
    postMessage(message, transfer)
  </CodeWindow>
</p-slide>
<p-slide layout="center">
  <CodeWindow is:raw lang="js" size="big">
    postMessage(message, transfer)
  </CodeWindow>
  <div layout="two-columns" style="margin-block-start: 1em">
    <div>
      <p>
        💬 <span gradient="sub">message</span>
      </p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="from left">
        📨 will be in the data field in the message object
      </p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
        🚩 required
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        🚚 <span gradient>transfer</span>
      </p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="from right">
        🚌 only transferable objects like ArrayBuffer or ImageBitmap
      </p>
      <p p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
        😶‍🌫️ optional
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      By default, when you use postMessage(message), the message is copied into
      the Worker's context (or the main thread) by structured cloning mechanism.
      But if you want to improve performance, you can transfer specific types of
      data instead of copying them
    </p>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
      <li>ArrayBuffer</li>
      <li>ImageBitmap</li>
      <li>OffscreenCanvas</li>
      <li>ReadableStream, WritableStream, TransformStream</li>
    </ul>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
{playful && (
  <WhoWins
    round={2}
    question="Which one logs 0?"
    winner="b"
    verdict="B logs 0: the buffer moved to the worker"
  >
    <CodeWindow is:raw slot="a" file="main.js" lang="js" dense>
        const buf = new ArrayBuffer(1e8);
        worker.postMessage(buf);
        console.log(buf.byteLength);
      </CodeWindow>
    <CodeWindow is:raw slot="b" file="main.js" lang="js" dense>
        const buf = new ArrayBuffer(1e8);
        worker.postMessage(buf, [buf]);
        console.log(buf.byteLength);
      </CodeWindow>
    <PNotes slot="notes">
      <p>Show of hands: A, B, or "both print 100000000"?</p>
      <p>
        A: structured clone, 100 MB copied, the main thread still owns its
        buffer → 100000000 (and you paid for the copy).
      </p>
      <p>
        B: transferred, zero-copy. The buffer is now detached on the main thread
        → byteLength is 0. Touching it afterwards is a bug.
      </p>
    </PNotes>
  </WhoWins>
)}
<p-slide>
  <CodeWindow is:raw lang="js" size="full">
    worker.onerror = (event) => {};
  </CodeWindow>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <CodeWindow is:raw lang="js" size="big">
    worker.onerror = (event) => {};
  </CodeWindow>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid" style="margin-block-start: 0.7em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      ⛔ ErrorEvent
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      💬 <span gradient="sub">message</span>, <code>filename</code>,{" "}
      <code>lineno</code>: what went wrong, and where
      <div style="margin-block-start: 0.5em">
        <CodeWindow is:raw lang="js">
          worker.onerror = (event) => {
            console.log(event.message);
          };
        </CodeWindow>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span aria-hidden="true">🤝🏻</span>
    <span gradient>Web Worker - Practical Use Cases</span>
  </h3>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid">
    <li>🖼️ Image compression and processing</li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from below">
      🧾 CSV data processing and formatting
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      🧮 Complex algorithms
    </li>
  </ul>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span gradient>Let's go further</span>
    <span aria-hidden="true">ᯓᯓ🏃🏻‍♀️‍➡️🏃🏻‍♀️‍➡️</span>
  </h3>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h2>
    <span aria-hidden="true">🤝🏻</span> <span gradient>Shared Worker</span>
  </h2>
  <PNotes>
    Dedicated Workers are the most common: They are created from a single page
    with new Worker(). Each instance is independent (if you open two tabs of the
    same app, you will have two distinct Web Workers). Perfect for operations
    tied only to the current page (e.g., file parsing or heavy calculations).
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span aria-hidden="true">🤝🏻</span> <span gradient>Shared Worker</span>
  </h3>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid">
    <li>
      🌍 <span gradient="sub">Can be shared</span> across multiple tabs of the
      same origin
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      😎 <span gradient="sub">Alive</span> while at least one tab is connected
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from below">
      💬 Communicate through the <span gradient="sub">MessagePort API</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      📱 <span gradient="sub">Back on Chrome for Android</span> in 2026
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Lifetime: the worker dies when its last connected document goes away. A
      single tab that reloads gets a brand new SharedWorker (state lost); it
      survives a reload only if another tab keeps it alive.
    </p>
    <p>
      MessagePort API:
      https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MessagePort : similar to
      postMessage but more powerful and flexible
    </p>
    <p>
      Android: SharedWorker was disabled for years over process-lifecycle
      concerns; Chrome re-enabled it starting from 148 beta (April 2026). Safari
      and Firefox have it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chrome origin trial: new SharedWorker(url, {"{"} extendedLifetime: true
      {"}"}) keeps it alive up to 30s after the last page unloads (save state,
      send analytics).
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span aria-hidden="true">🤝🏻</span>
    <span gradient>Shared Worker - Practical Use Cases</span>
  </h3>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid">
    <li>
      🗣️ Cross-tab communication through the{" "}
      <span gradient="sub">MessagePort API</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🗃️ Shared Cache or Data Storage
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      👥 Session Management
    </li>
  </ul>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow is:raw file="shared-worker.js" lang="js" scale={0.76} indent={4}>
    const clients = [];

    self.onconnect = (event) => {
      const [port] = event.ports;
      clients.push(port);
      port.postMessage("Worker connected");
      port.onmessage = (e) => {
        clients.forEach((client) =>
          client.postMessage(`Broadcast: "${e.data}"`)
        );
      };
    };
  </CodeWindow>
  <p-fragment>
    <span
      sketch-circle
      style="--sketch-height: 1.4em; position: absolute; inset-block-end: 8.3em; inline-size: 8em; inset-inline-start: 1.9em; z-index: 1"
    ></span>
  </p-fragment>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow is:raw file="main.js" lang="js" indent={4}>
    const sharedWorker = new SharedWorker("shared-worker.js");
    sharedWorker.port.start();

    sharedWorker.port.onmessage = (e) => {
      console.log(e.data);
    };

    sharedWorker.port.postMessage("Hello from Tab 1");
  </CodeWindow>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      port.start() is strictly needed only with addEventListener("message").
      Assigning port.onmessage starts the port implicitly; calling start()
      anyway is harmless and makes the intent explicit.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
  <p-fragment>
    <span
      sketch-circle
      style="--sketch-height: 1.8em; position: absolute; inset-block-end: 8.3em; inline-size: 5em; inset-inline-start: 5.9em; z-index: 1"
    ></span>
  </p-fragment>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h2>
    <span aria-hidden="true">⛓️‍💥</span>
    <span gradient>When a client disconnects</span>
  </h2>
</p-slide>
{playful && (
  <WhoWins
    round={3}
    question="Which one notices a closed tab?"
    winner="b"
    verdict="B wins: the close event never shipped"
  >
    <CodeWindow is:raw slot="a" file="shared-worker.js" lang="js" dense>
        port.onclose = () => {
          clients.splice(
            clients.indexOf(port), 1
          );
        };
      </CodeWindow>
    <CodeWindow is:raw slot="b" file="main.js" lang="js" dense>
        addEventListener("pagehide", () => {
          sharedWorker.port
            .postMessage("bye");
        });
      </CodeWindow>
    <PNotes slot="notes">
      <p>
        Show of hands first. A looks right: a close event on MessagePort was
        added to the HTML spec a few years ago.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trap: no browser ever shipped it (Chrome only behind a test flag),
        and on 2 October 2026 it was removed from the spec (whatwg/html #13016).
        A never fires.
      </p>
      <p>
        B: the page says goodbye itself. Caveats: pagehide also fires when the
        page goes into the back/forward cache (say hello again on pageshow when
        event.persisted), and it never fires on a crash, so a heartbeat is the
        belt-and-braces option.
      </p>
    </PNotes>
  </WhoWins>
)}
<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow
    is:raw
    file="shared-worker.js"
    lang="js"
    scale={0.92}
    tail={0}
    fragments="5-9"
  >
    self.onconnect = (event) => {
      const [port] = event.ports;
      clients.push(port);
      port.onmessage = ({ data }) => {
        if (data !== "bye") return; // ...other messages
        clients.splice(clients.indexOf(port), 1);
        clients.forEach((client) =>
          client.postMessage(`Clients left: ${clients.length}`)
        );
      };
    };
  </CodeWindow>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      There is no reliable "port closed" event: MessagePort's close event was
      removed from the HTML spec in October 2026, no browser shipped it.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the client says goodbye on pagehide and the worker removes it from the
      list, then broadcasts the new count to the remaining clients.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
{!playful && (
  <p-slide>
    <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
      Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
    </h4>
    <CodeWindow is:raw file="main.js" lang="js">
        addEventListener("pagehide", () => {
          sharedWorker.port.postMessage("bye");
        });
      </CodeWindow>
  </p-slide>
)}
<p-slide layout="center">
  <h2>
    <span gradient>Web Worker limitations</span>
    <span aria-hidden="true">❌</span>
  </h2>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid">
    <li>
      ❌ <span gradient="sub">No access to the DOM</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      ❌{" "}
      <span gradient="sub">
        No <code style="font-size: 0.8em">window</code>,{" "}
        <code style="font-size: 0.8em">document</code> or{" "}
        <code style="font-size: 0.8em">parent</code>
      </span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      ❌{" "}
      <span gradient="sub">
        No <code style="font-size: 0.8em">alert()</code>,{" "}
        <code style="font-size: 0.8em">confirm()</code> or{" "}
        <code style="font-size: 0.8em">prompt()</code>
      </span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      ❌{" "}
      <span gradient="sub">
        No <code style="font-size: 0.8em">localStorage</code> or{" "}
        <code style="font-size: 0.8em">sessionStorage</code>
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
      <li>
        ❌ localStorage, sessionStorage and document.cookie are not available
        (they are synchronous). ✅ IndexedDB, the Cache API and OPFS work fine
        in workers, and OPFS createSyncAccessHandle() works only in a worker.
      </li>
      <li>
        Modules: classic workers load scripts with importScripts()
        (synchronous); module workers, new Worker(url, {"{"} type: 'module'
        {"}"}), use import instead and importScripts() throws there.
      </li>
      <li>
        ❌ Restricted access to certain JavaScript APIs - Web Workers cannot use
        WebRTC, CanvasRenderingContext2D (except OffscreenCanvas), some
        security-related APIs, and other browser-specific features.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
